%I A401445 #8 Nov 02 2026 19:28:50
%S A401445 29,157,377,1061,1717,1241,3677,2581,6817,7841,11497,11981,15541,8177,
%T A401445 24137,19541,27157,37157,19981,39097,43877,63901,54497,75961,73697,
%U A401445 56501,73957,103121,102917,57097,76157,122497,159421,148817,176677,157781,205717,175961
%N A401445 Half the sum of the squares after one Fibonacci recurrence step (v, u) -> (u, u+v): a(n) = (u^2 + (u+v)^2)/2, where u and v are the odd and even legs of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse A002144(n).
%C A401445 For p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, the odd leg is u = x^2 - y^2 and the even leg is v = 2*x*y. Either leg may be the longer one.
%C A401445 Let s(0) = v, s(1) = u and s(k+2) = s(k+1) + s(k). This is the recurrence satisfied by the Fibonacci numbers A000045, with different initial values. One step takes (v, u) to (u, u+v), so s(2) = u+v and a(n) = (s(1)^2 + s(2)^2)/2.
%C A401445 Each prime p supplies a new pair of initial values. Terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of a(n); for example, a(5) = 1717 > a(6) = 1241.
%C A401445 A401444 lists the Pythagorean primes p for which a(n) is prime. Every prime factor of a(n) is congruent to 1 modulo 4; see the linked note.
%C A401445 For p = A002144(n), let Q = p*a(n). The construction in A401446 gives a 3 X 3 magic square with central entry Q^2 and nine distinct positive integer entries, at least six of them squares. This works whether a(n) is prime or composite.
%H A401445 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401445/b401445.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A401445 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A401445 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A401445 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A401445 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A401445 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401445 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401445 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A401445 If p = A002144(n) and u, v are its odd and even legs, then a(n) = (u^2 + (u+v)^2)/2 = (u+v/2)^2 + (v/2)^2.
%F A401445 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then a(n) = (x^2 - y^2 + x*y)^2 + x^2*y^2 = x^4 + 2*x^3*y - 2*x*y^3 + y^4.
%F A401445 a(n) = (A070079(n) + 2*A070151(n))^2 + 4*A070151(n)^2.
%F A401445 Here A070079(n) is the odd leg and A070151(n) is one quarter of the even leg.
%e A401445 The first Pythagorean prime is 5, whose primitive triangle has odd leg 3 and even leg 4. Thus a(1) = (3^2 + 7^2)/2 = 29.
%e A401445 The next two Pythagorean primes are 13 and 17, with odd and even legs (5, 12) and (15, 8), respectively. Consequently, a(2) = (5^2 + 17^2)/2 = 157 and a(3) = (15^2 + 23^2)/2 = 377 = 13*29.
%e A401445 For p = 29, the odd leg is 21 and the even leg is 20, so a(4) = (21^2 + 41^2)/2 = 1061.
%o A401445 (Python)
%o A401445 from math import isqrt
%o A401445 from sympy import primerange
%o A401445 def a401445(limit=500):
%o A401445     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A401445     out = []
%o A401445     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A401445         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A401445             continue
%o A401445         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A401445             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A401445             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A401445                 u, w = x*x - y*y, x*y
%o A401445                 # u is the odd leg; w is half the even leg.
%o A401445                 q = (u+w)**2 + w*w
%o A401445                 out.append(q)
%o A401445                 break
%o A401445     return out
%Y A401445 Cf. A000045, A002144, A002365, A002366, A070079, A070151, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401446.
